        "authors": [
          "Martin Davis",
          "Hilary Putnam"
        ],
        "relationship": "Classical variable-elimination framework underlying selector projection bounds; cited in PR #46 and integrated here for coupled selectors.",
        "priority_evidence": "Published in Journal of the ACM 7(3), pp. 201-215 (1960)."
      },
      {
        "reference": "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-81955-1_28",
        "title": "On the Complexity of Derivation in Propositional Calculus",
        "authors": [
          "Grigori S. Tseitin"
        ],
        "relationship": "Classical source for auxiliary extension variables in CNF and propositional proof systems; the width-3 splitting of C_0 uses standard Tseitin variables without claiming priority.",
        "priority_evidence": "Originally 1968 LOMI seminar; English translation in Automation of Reasoning 2, Springer, pp. 466-483 (1983)."
      },
      {
        "reference": "https://doi.org/10.2307/2273702",
        "title": "The relative efficiency of propositional proof systems",
        "authors": [
          "Stephen A. Cook",
          "Robert A. Reckhow"
        ],
        "relationship": "Classical framework separating proof/representation size from soundness; contextualizes the comparison across auxiliary 3-CNF, retained CNF, and row certificates.",
        "priority_evidence": "Published in Journal of Symbolic Logic 44(1), pp. 36-50 (1979)."
      }
    ],
    "discovery_basis": "Inspected all 4 live ledger records via pnp list and read existing submissions and notes on MuncleUscles/p-vs-np:research (including PR #46, PR #22, PR #19, PR #14), plus verified DOIs for Davis-Putnam (1960), Tseitin (1968/1983), and Cook-Reckhow (1979). This is quantitative integration and extension of repository bounds, not a claim to originate Tseitin compression or DNF-to-CNF blowup."
  },
  "evidence": {
    "kind": "research-note",
    "artifact_path": "research/notes/coupled-selector-cnf-separation.md",
    "reproduce": "Run pnp format and pnp check on research/submissions/coupled-selector-cnf-separation.json for schema validation, and execute the standalone Python 3.13.15 script in research/notes/coupled-selector-cnf-separation.md to reproduce all projection and exhaustive minimality checks."
  },
  "dependencies": [],
  "attribution": {
    "wallet": "0x97f0A5B60D9edC6D3c78f1275B3CE767384a08D3",
    "contributions": "GitHub contributor Amarudinn (assisted by an AI agent) derived the coupled multi-selector projection proof, the fooling-set lower bound for arbitrary signed list-CNFs, the linear 3-CNF encoding count, and ran the Python 3.13.15 verification script. Prior repository work and classical papers are explicitly cited."
  },
  "limitations": "This is a nonformal research note, not a Lean-checked theorem. The prod(m_r) clause lower bound applies to pointwise Boolean equivalence over the retained variables without auxiliary variables; allowing auxiliary variables yields linear size. Finite Python verification covers k = 1..4 (m_r = 2) and exhaustive general-CNF minimality up to M = 4; general parameters rely on the written mathematical proof. No general resolution lower bound, Extended Resolution lower bound, or P-versus-NP claim is made."
}
